# Build quality, formatting, and the fix loop

Covers formatting, linting, test generation/execution, and the self-correcting fix loop — the capabilities deciding how thoroughly a project is verified before the agent stops, and how it self-corrects when verification fails.

## Formatting guard

One shared block, injected into every one of the 11 target builders: *"Write real, properly indented multi-line files — NEVER collapse content onto a single line, even a short config file. A literal `\n` (backslash + "n") is NOT a line break."* Exists because generated projects were observed shipping files collapsed onto one line — the model imitating a prompt's own inline shorthand as literal file content.

## Linting

Normalized before it ever reaches a prompt builder: `normalize_linting_tool(tool, language, framework)` degrades an unrecognized value to `"none"` (step skipped) with a logged substitution event — the same shape as the testing-framework model below. This closes the old failure mode where an unrecognized value silently produced no lint step at all, no error, no log.

Recognized per ecosystem:

| Mode(s) | Recognizes | Step # |
|---|---|---|
| FastAPI, Django, Tornado (greenfield) | `ruff` | 11.5 |
| Flask, Bottle | `flake8`, `ruff` | 10.5 |
| Node.js greenfield | `eslint` | 10.5 |
| Java (monolith, microservices, brownfield) | `checkstyle` | varies |
| Python brownfield | `ruff` | 5.5 |
| Node.js brownfield | `eslint` | 5.5 |

Node.js brownfield now has its own recognized set (it used to route through the Python brownfield builder, which only knew `ruff`, so it couldn't lint at all — fixed by giving it the same dedicated builder treatment as its other steps; see [Node.js brownfield](../modes/brownfield/02-nodejs.md)).

## Testing framework resolution

One normalizer resolves `testing_framework` before any builder runs, closing two separate failure modes that used to exist independently:

- **Silent step loss** (Flask/Bottle/Node.js greenfield, Python/Node.js brownfield): an unrecognized value used to leave the entire testing STEP absent from the rendered prompt, with no event — and because the completion gate still applies whenever `testing_framework != "none"`, that produced a job gated on a report the agent was never told to write.
- **Generation/execution disagreement** (FastAPI): the generation step and the execution step used to recognize different sets, so an unrecognized value could get test files written that were never run.

Now every mode's generation and execution steps consult the same already-resolved value. An unsupported request (e.g. `schemathesis` on a framework with no OpenAPI schema by default) degrades to `curl` with a logged substitution event — the model both Django and Tornado already used correctly.

| Language / framework default | Value |
|---|---|
| Java microservices | `junit` |
| Java monolith/brownfield, Django, Flask, Bottle, Node.js | `curl` |
| FastAPI, Tornado | `none` |

`"none"` skips the server-start step entirely — the two async Python frameworks keep that default for historical compatibility, everything else defaults to at least a boot-and-probe. That one default is the biggest determinant of whether the completion gate applies to a given job at all (see [Quality Gates](../reference/07-quality-gates.md)).

## Java testing

Shared JUnit/curl blocks used by both Java greenfield modes and Java brownfield's fix-loop: unit-test additions, integration-test additions (+ an auth-aware variant), an enforcement note explicitly rejecting "Tests run: 0" as a pass, endpoint-test additions, and a microservices-only inter-service test addition that greps for cross-service call sites and requires a real test hitting the already-running callee.

## Fix loop

The self-correction contract, shared across modes:

- **Iteration cap**: up to 5 iterations everywhere it applies. Flask/Bottle's loop text is flat ("repeat until all pass") rather than itemizing the cap explicitly, but the same 5-iteration ceiling governs it.
- **Recall-first discipline**: wherever MemClaw tools are wired, the first action on any failure MUST be a recall of similar past fixes before forming an own diagnosis; a confirmed fix is recorded once per distinct bug.
- **Stop conditions**: zero failures/timeouts remain and every skip is confirmed genuinely untestable, OR every remaining failure has a concrete external cause, OR the iteration cap is reached with no further reduction.
- **Java's three-arm dispatch** (shared by both Java greenfield modes): `"none"` → compile-check only; `"curl"` → inline curl, no `.sh` files, restart by PID; else (`"junit"`, the real default for both) → JUnit 5 + Mockito with the "Tests run: 0 is not a pass" enforcement note. All three arms boot the app itself the same way — the shared start-script mechanism in [Reports & Deploy](reports-and-deploy.md#boot-verification-every-mode-every-language), restarted/re-tested by PID each fix-loop iteration — never by rebuilding and re-`up`-ing a per-service container.
- **Microservices scoping**: the fix loop restarts and re-tests only the failing module, never a batched multi-module command — a batched invocation makes it easy to silently drop one module's failures.
- **Tornado's fix-loop text** no longer carries Django's "re-run migrations if a model changed" line unconditionally — that line is now conditional on the ORM actually having a migrations step, since Tornado has none.

## The completion-gate contract (what report generation actually specifies)

Every mode's report-generation step produces the same workbook shape — see [Reports & Deploy](reports-and-deploy.md) for the mechanics, this is the contract itself:

- Columns: `#, Method, Endpoint, Description, Status Code, Pass/Fail, Reason` (Java microservices prepends a `Service` column).
- Pass/Fail must contain the literal word `PASS` (case-insensitive `PASSED` also tolerated — nothing else counts).
- PASS = a 2xx response, or a deliberately-tested rejection 4xx (e.g. 401 on a protected route with no token).
- FAIL = a should've-succeeded 4xx, any 5xx, a refused connection, a timeout, or a non-zero test-script exit.
- SKIPPED is recorded as FAIL with `Reason = "Skipped: <exact reason>"` — there is no separate SKIPPED status the gate treats leniently.
- One row per real, individually-verified check only — no synthetic or aggregated rows. Most rows are a real HTTP request; a genuine non-HTTP verification (a Kafka consumer actually joining its group and consuming a message, a message-broker delivery check, a JUnit suite's own result) is also a legitimate row, but never dress it up as an HTTP call to fit the shape: leave Status Code blank (or `N/A`) — there is no HTTP status to report — and leave Endpoint blank too rather than putting a topic/queue/suite name where a URL path belongs; put exactly what was actually observed (e.g. "consumer joined group and consumed 1 message") in Description/Reason instead.
- A row's Status Code and Pass/Fail must come from a response object the report script itself received when it made that request — never authored by predicting what the implementation should return. This holds regardless of tech stack, greenfield or brownfield: a row with no real request behind it is a contract violation even if its Pass/Fail value happens to be correct.
- **Never drop a previously-recorded unique test case.** If this job's report-generation step runs more than once in the same job (e.g. an interim check during the fix loop, then the final write), each run must preserve every row already established — re-test it and update its Status Code/Pass-Fail/Reason if it's retestable this run, otherwise leave it exactly as last recorded — and add newly-discovered cases on top. Never regenerate the workbook from a fresh, independent rediscovery of endpoints/checks that silently drops a row this job already established just because this particular run didn't happen to re-detect it; a shrinking report is as much a contract violation as a fabricated row. This also protects the platform's own report-repair fallback, which reconstructs `api_test_report.xlsx` from this same script's `test_results.json` write when the xlsx write is missing — see [Task Pipeline](../reference/06-task-pipeline.md) — so that file must carry the same accumulated, never-shrinking set.
- **Only include a test case a correct implementation can actually pass.** Don't manufacture a foregone-conclusion FAIL by testing a route in a setup that structurally can't succeed regardless of whether the code is right — see [Reports & Deploy](reports-and-deploy.md#boot-verification-every-mode-every-language) for the specific case (a downstream-dependent route probed before its dependency is up) and the fix (sequence or drop that probe, don't report it as a finding about the app).
- If the thing a row would report on never actually ran — the server never booted, the test run never executed, the curl transcript was never captured — that row is FAIL with `Reason = "Skipped: <exact reason>"` per the rule above. A failed boot or failed test run is never a reason to fall back to predicted rows instead of real ones; see [Reports & Deploy](reports-and-deploy.md)'s boot-verification section for what happens upstream of this.
- **Omitting a row is not a safer alternative to fabricating one — write the Skipped row, don't leave it out.** Confirmed failure mode: never attempting the full-stack boot at all (not even a failed attempt — genuinely never issuing the start-script call), then reasoning "I won't fabricate HTTP/Kafka rows for something I never ran" and simply not writing them, leaving a workbook containing only JUnit-suite rows, every one `PASS`. That workbook reads as a clean, complete pass to anyone — or any automated gate — reading only the Pass/Fail column; it is *more* misleading than an honest `Skipped: <exact reason>` row would have been, not a more conservative choice. For Java specifically, this matters even on the `"junit"` arm: per [Java testing](#java-testing) above, all three arms boot the app the same way, so "the JUnit suite passed" is never itself a substitute for the endpoint/Kafka rows the boot-verification phase was supposed to produce — if that phase never ran, its rows still belong in the workbook, marked FAIL/Skipped with a real reason, not silently absent.
- Before writing a single `Skipped: ...` row, confirm to yourself that the start-script call in [Reports & Deploy](reports-and-deploy.md#boot-verification-every-mode-every-language) was actually issued (up to its 3-attempt retry limit) — not merely that infra came up, or that something else went wrong while you were getting ready to test. A `Skipped` reason must name how many boot attempts were made and quote the last captured error; a reason that only states the outcome, with no attempt count or captured error, reports a step that was never tried, not one that failed, and is itself a contract violation. Getting distracted by an unrelated failure (a helper script blocked for its own reasons, a slow container, a wrong path) and quietly writing every row `Skipped` instead of returning to actually attempt the boot is the single most common way this rule gets violated in practice.
