# Reports, boot verification, and CI/infra scaffolding

The completion-gate *contract* (workbook columns, PASS/FAIL rules) is in [Build, Quality & Testing](build-quality-and-testing.md), shared with the fix loop. This file covers the mechanics: how the reports actually get written, how a server gets booted and re-booted, and optional unit-test/CI scaffolding.

## Reports — one canonical mechanism per ecosystem, used everywhere in it

Two artifacts, always generated together: an xlsx test report and a docx summary. **Greenfield**: STEP 15/15.5 for FastAPI/Django/Tornado/Java monolith, STEP 16/16.5 for Flask/Bottle/Node.js, STEP 11/11.5 for Java microservices — mode-local numbers don't need to match, see [Prompt Architecture](../reference/04-prompt-architecture.md). **Brownfield**: STEP 12/12.5, plus a deploy-script step at STEP 13.

- **`api_test_report.xlsx`** — one canonical, verbatim script embed per ecosystem, not a mix of embedded-script/prose-description/reimplementation:
  - **Python/openpyxl** script, used by every Python-family greenfield mode, both Java greenfield modes, and both Python and Java brownfield. (Java projects use this Python throwaway script too — it's a report-generation tool, not part of the generated app, so the app's own language is irrelevant to it.)
  - **Node/exceljs** script, used by both Node.js greenfield and Node.js brownfield — promoted from what used to be a Node.js-brownfield-only reimplementation to the shared mechanism for both Node modes, closing the old bug where Node.js greenfield instead got the Python script's prose (telling it to `pip install` and run `python3`).
  - Small per-caller deltas only: extra directories to skip when scanning the tree (Java adds `target/`, `build/`, `.gradle/`), and whether the source-of-truth for endpoints is a curl transcript or a test run.
  - In every case, the script itself is the thing that issues each request (over HTTP, against the booted server) and reads the row's Status Code/Pass off the response it gets back — it is a live test runner that happens to also emit a workbook, not a template that gets filled in from something else. See [Build, Quality & Testing](build-quality-and-testing.md)'s completion-gate contract for why this isn't optional.
- **`project_report.docx`** (greenfield) / **`changes_report.docx`** (brownfield, parses the change log + the xlsx) — python-docx, uniform across every mode including Node.js brownfield, which used to be the one brownfield mode with no docx step at all.

Both helper scripts are one-off: written, run, verified to exist, then deleted — never meant to survive into the delivered project (the push step's `.gitignore` hardening excludes them explicitly as a backstop). Both artifacts (`api_test_report.xlsx`, `project_report.docx`/`changes_report.docx`) land under a `tests-artifacts/` subfolder of the delivered project, not its root, in every mode — greenfield and brownfield alike — so the root of a delivered project shows application code first, not generation-time tooling output.

**Email placement**: the email-report step is now positioned in every mode right after the docx step, since it attaches the xlsx — it no longer textually precedes the artifact it references.

## Boot verification (every mode, every language)

One mechanism, uniform across brownfield and greenfield alike, and across Python/Node.js/Java: the generated application's **own** services are started via a **start script that launches them as backgrounded processes**, tracked by PID, and killed by PID — never by bringing up per-service containers via `docker compose up --build`.

- **Where the start script actually runs**: natively via `execute` when the stack's runtime is on the host (this sandbox has Python natively, but not Node.js or a JDK — see [skills/docker-toolchain](../skills/docker-toolchain/SKILL.md)); otherwise as one `docker_exec` call whose `command` *is* the start script — build once if needed, launch every service backgrounded inside that same call, poll health, then either test from inside that same call (client and server share one container, so plain `localhost` reaches them) or keep the processes alive long enough for a host-side test/report script, via `docker_exec`'s `ports` argument publishing whatever ports that script needs. Because the container is discarded (`--rm`) the moment the call returns, a background process cannot outlive the call that started it — the command itself must start, wait on, test, and stop them.
- **Infra dependencies** (a database, a broker, a cache — anything not generated code) are the one thing that legitimately still runs via `docker compose up`/`down` — see [Container image policy](#container-image-policy-docker-compose--dockerfiles-every-mode) below. When the app's own start script needs to reach that infra by its compose service name, the `docker_exec` call above passes `network` set to that compose project's network.
- **Infra being up is never a substitute for the app's own boot.** Bringing up a database/broker container and then moving straight to report-writing — without ever issuing the start-script call in step 3 below — is a real, observed failure mode, not a hypothetical one: it produces a report that reads as an honest "Skipped: boot was not completed" but actually reflects zero boot attempts rather than a genuine failure. Infra-up is a *precondition* for step 3, never a stand-in for it.
- **The generated `Dockerfile` per service, and any app-service entries in `docker-compose.yml`, are flag-gated deliverables** for whoever deploys the finished project (per-mode "Infra" STEPs, e.g. Java monolith's STEP 7, Java microservices' STEP 5.5) — real and correct, but never the mechanism this harness's own verification depends on. A job never fails, retries, or blocks on whether that Dockerfile happens to build; it only depends on the start script succeeding.
- **Never probe a downstream-dependent route before its dependency is up.** In microservices mode especially, a route that legitimately calls another service (a gateway routing to a business service, a service calling a peer) will correctly 5xx/refuse if tested in isolation, before that peer is booted — that failure describes the test's own setup, not the generated app, and reporting it as a `FAIL` row manufactures a foregone-conclusion failure rather than a finding. The start script must bring up every service this route depends on (in dependency order — see each mode's own multi-service boot ordering, e.g. Java microservices' Eureka → business services → gateway last) before that route is exercised; if a mode's test step still needs an isolated single-service probe for some other reason, that probe's result is not a substitute for testing the route with its real dependency running, and must not be the row the completion-gate contract's Pass/Fail column reflects for it.

Runtime steps, uniform across every mode:

1. Kill any existing instance of the app's own processes (by previously-recorded PID; never a broad port-kill beyond the app's own allocated ports).
2. When running via `docker_exec` (runtime not on the host): before anything else in that call, run the detect-then-install idiom in [skills/docker-toolchain](../skills/docker-toolchain/SKILL.md) for whatever verification client the image doesn't ship — `curl` is never preinstalled in the images this harness uses, but which package manager is actually present varies by image, so detect it rather than assuming. Skipping this step is what produces `Reason = "Skipped: ... required client tool unavailable"` rows below — that reason is an avoidable dependency gap, not a legitimate skip, and must not recur.
3. Start the start script backgrounded (natively, or via the `docker_exec` pattern above). **This call is mandatory, not optional, and "Skipped" is never a substitute for making it.** Every one of the 3 retry attempts in step 6 below means this call was actually issued that many times — not that the agent reasoned about why it might fail and decided against trying. If anything else goes wrong while preparing for or wrapping up verification — a report-writer helper script failing for an unrelated reason (blocked by a host security policy, a missing package, a wrong working directory), a slow infra container, an unrelated tool error — that is a problem to solve or route around, never a reason to abandon this step and fall through to writing Skipped rows. Resolve the distraction (switch tool, fix the script, wait longer) and come back to this step; do not let it stand in for a boot attempt that never happened.
4. Poll health up to 120s (Python/Node.js) / 180s (Java).
5. On success: kill the boot-verify process once verification is done — a live deploy tunnel (where one exists) re-runs the start independently, so killing here is correct, not wasteful.
6. On failure: diagnose with captured stderr, retry up to 3 attempts total, then continue anyway — a failed boot-verify is logged, not a hard stop. "Continue anyway" means continue the deploy/report pipeline, not fabricate the report: every endpoint row whose source-of-truth depended on this boot is still FAIL with `Reason = "Skipped: <exact reason>"`, per [Build, Quality & Testing](build-quality-and-testing.md)'s completion-gate contract — a boot-verify failure here is exactly the case that rule exists for. A missing client tool (step 2) is not one of these legitimate failure causes; treat it as a bug in the start script and fix it, not a reason to skip. `<exact reason>` must itself prove an attempt happened: name the attempt count (e.g. "3/3 attempts") and quote the actual captured stderr/exit code from the last one — a reason that only describes an outcome ("boot was not completed in this environment") with no attempt count or captured error attached is indistinguishable from never having tried, and must be treated as a contract violation, not a legitimate skip.
7. **Tear down this job's own infra containers — `docker_compose(workdir, "down")` — before the final summary. This is mandatory whenever step 3 or the fix loop ever ran `docker compose up` for this job's infra, not optional, and not something to leave for "later" since there is no later.** A missing teardown is a real, observed failure mode, not a hypothetical one: infra services bind fixed, conventional host ports (a database's, a broker's — e.g. Kafka's `9092`, Oracle's `1521`), not a per-job dynamically-allocated port the way the app's own services get — so a container left running after one job silently breaks the *next* job's boot verification with a port-bind conflict at container startup, which then cascades into unrelated-looking timeouts and FAIL rows for a completely different project. That failure is easy to misdiagnose as a code or infra problem in the job that actually failed, when the real cause is a previous job's uncompleted cleanup. Tearing down is the same `docker_compose(workdir, "down")` call regardless of how boot verification went — success, retries exhausted, or anything in between.

Node.js brownfield gets the same retry loop as Python/Java — it used to just write and `chmod` the script with no runtime check at all.

## Container image policy (docker-compose / Dockerfiles, every mode)

Every image a generated project's `docker-compose.yml` or Dockerfile pulls — a database (Oracle, Postgres, MySQL, MongoDB, ...), a broker (Kafka, RabbitMQ, ...), a cache (Redis, ...), or anything else infra-shaped — must be freely pullable: no paid tier, no registry login, no vendor account. This is a target-design rule, not a harness quirk: a generated project that only boots for someone who happens to have paid registry credentials is broken for everyone else who tries to run it.

- **Oracle**: use `gvenzl/oracle-free` (or `gvenzl/oracle-xe` for an older target) from Docker Hub, never `container-registry.oracle.com/database/...` — the official Oracle registry requires an authenticated login and fails boot verification outright with an HTTP 401, which is not a fixable error, just the wrong image.
- The same test applies to every other engine/broker/cache choice: if pulling the image a project's compose file names would prompt for credentials or a paid plan, it's the wrong image for that role — substitute the nearest free, unauthenticated equivalent instead of accepting the failure downstream (and letting the completion-gate contract above turn every dependent row into a boot-failure `FAIL`).
- When `database.engine` (or an equivalent config field) names a vendor whose free image differs from its paid one in meaningful ways — a smaller feature set, a different default port, a different startup/readiness signature — note that substitution once in the mode's own STEP output rather than silently pretending it's the vendor's full paid product.

## API run / test execution (greenfield)

Covers the actual test-execution instructions once test files exist, for FastAPI, Django, and Tornado. FastAPI's curl branch enforces ngrok-first ordering (start ngrok → patch env → start server → run tests — never reversed). Django and Tornado share a start-server helper (backgrounded, logged, any HTTP status counts as "up") and both warn against foregrounding `start.sh` or waiting on the server PID combined with a kill, since their dev servers never return control to the shell. FastAPI additionally writes an Azure-DevOps-deferred Dockerfile once every test has passed, requiring `EXPOSE 8000` (Container Apps' fixed target port, independent of the job's allocated port).

Flask, Bottle, and Node.js run their own test-execution text as part of their testing-framework step instead (see [Build, Quality & Testing](build-quality-and-testing.md)). Brownfield doesn't use this section either — each brownfield mode's own server-start-and-test step is self-contained. Neither Java mode has its own test-execution prose at all; both point at the "Boot verification" section above for the mechanics (start script, never `docker compose up --build` for the app's own services) and at [Build, Quality & Testing](build-quality-and-testing.md#java-testing) for the JUnit/curl content itself.

## Optional infra scaffolding (greenfield)

Three independent, flag-gated additions, available to FastAPI/Django/Tornado (not Flask/Bottle/Node.js/Java):

- **Unit tests**: pure-logic tests (validators, calculations, model methods), no running server, explicitly separate from the API-level suite. Django's variant marks DB-touching cases appropriately for its test runner.
- **CI workflow**: writes a CI config — checkout, language setup, install, a compile/check scan, plus a unit-test step if unit tests are also enabled. Self-contained — no secrets or external services required.
- **Azure DevOps Dockerfile deferral**: when Azure DevOps is enabled, FastAPI's normal Dockerfile write is replaced with a note deferring it to the API-run step above, since the smoke-tested Dockerfile Azure DevOps needs is a different shape than the plain one would otherwise produce.

Every one of these flags is honored exactly as requested — a mode that doesn't explicitly gate on one of them silently doesn't offer it. Check what a new mode gates on before assuming it inherits these for free.
