# Docker toolchain

Gives the coding agent a way to "install" and use a tech stack's runtime — Python, Node.js, or a JDK — as a disposable container, instead of installing it onto the host OS. Every container is ephemeral (`--rm`); nothing of the container itself persists. Two things do persist across calls, deliberately: the pulled image layers, and — mounted as a named Docker volume, auto-managed by `docker_exec`, keyed by which ecosystem `image` names — that ecosystem's package-manager cache (`~/.gradle`, `~/.m2`, pip's cache, npm's cache). Without that second one, every single call would re-download the whole dependency tree from scratch; with it, only the first call in a job pays that cost. A third thing can persist too, if you build it yourself: a derived image layering an extra client tool (`curl`, `jq`, ...) onto a base image once, instead of installing it fresh in every container that needs it — see "Never assume a client tool is preinstalled" below.

**A persistent cache shared across ephemeral, separately-killed containers has one hazard, already handled for you**: each container's PID namespace resets to 1, so if an earlier call gets killed mid-build (a boot-verification timeout, a fix-loop retry) before its package manager releases a lock file in that shared cache, a *later* container's own process can end up with a coincidentally-reused PID that makes the stale lock look like it's still held by something alive — deadlocking every later call that touches the same cache entries. Confirmed in practice for Gradle (`*.lock` under `~/.gradle/caches`, surfaced as a boot-verification attempt failing with a file-hash-cache-lock error after a previous timed-out attempt). `docker_exec` clears each ecosystem's own known stale-lock/partial-transfer files automatically before every call now, so this doesn't need a workaround in the command you write — just know that if you ever see a lock-related error from Gradle/Maven/npm/pip anyway, it's an infrastructure symptom of this class, not something your code caused.

**Every `docker_exec` call also transparently sets each ecosystem's own network timeout/retry knobs** (Gradle: `GRADLE_OPTS`; Maven: `MAVEN_OPTS`; npm: `npm_config_fetch_*` env vars; pip: `/etc/pip.conf`) — a short (~60s) per-attempt timeout with a few retries, instead of the only safety net being `docker_exec`'s own flat, whole-call timeout with no retry at all, which meant one hung dependency-repository connection could silently burn the entire call before failing. Gradle's own daemon is also disabled this way (`-Dorg.gradle.daemon=false`): a background daemon is pure overhead in a container that's discarded the moment the call returns, and was observed to occasionally leave a container unable to exit cleanly. None of this needs anything added to the command you write.

**One Gradle-specific gotcha worth knowing even though it's already handled**: the official `gradle:<version>-jdk<n>-alpine` Docker Hub image (as opposed to running the Gradle wrapper on top of a plain `eclipse-temurin` JDK image) bakes `/root/.gradle` as a symlink to `/home/gradle/.gradle`, and separately declares `/home/gradle/.gradle` as its own Dockerfile `VOLUME` — an ordinary volume mount at `/root/.gradle` never reaches the real directory, so every call silently got a throwaway anonymous volume instead, discarding the *entire* Gradle cache (wrapper distribution and dependency cache alike) every single time, with no visible error. `docker_exec` now mounts the cache volume at both paths, so this works regardless of which of the two Java base images ends up used — but if you're ever choosing between them for a *new* derived image, prefer `eclipse-temurin` + the Gradle wrapper (per the image table above): it doesn't have this footgun and doesn't need a pinned Gradle version baked into the image at all, since the wrapper handles that.

## Why containers instead of host installs

The host running this agent is not provisioned with all 11 tech-stack runtimes, and won't be — installing/uninstalling language runtimes on the host for each job is slow, leaves drift behind, and one job's toolchain (say, an old Node version) shouldn't collide with another's. A pinned, minimal image per stack sidesteps all of that: pull once, run many times, leave no trace on the host beyond the image cache.

**This is a per-host default, not a hard requirement, and it's a toggle, not something you as the agent choose per call.** When `SDLC_AGENT_EXECUTION_MODE=sandbox` (a host-level setting — see `.env.example`), `docker_exec` transparently runs the exact same command directly on the host instead, whenever a matching native toolchain is already on PATH, falling back to the normal containerized path per call otherwise (missing/mismatched toolchain, or any call passing `network`, since only a real container network can reach compose infra by service name). This is invisible to you: `docker_exec`'s contract, arguments, and return format never change — write commands exactly as this skill already describes either way. It exists for a host that's specifically provisioned for the stacks it generates (e.g. a persistent Ubuntu worker with the right JDK/Node/Python already installed), where a native process is faster precisely *because* its package-manager cache is an ordinary, already-persistent directory in `$HOME` rather than something a `--rm` container has to be specially configured not to discard.

**"Minimal" is a hard preference, not a suggestion**: always pick the smallest official image that still has the tools a step needs (`-slim` / `-alpine` variants), never a full desktop/OS-flavored base, and never `:latest` — pin a real tag so a job run today behaves the same next month.

## Image per tech-stack mode

| Mode(s) | Image | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| FastAPI, Django, Tornado, Flask, Bottle (greenfield); Python (brownfield) | `python:3.13-slim` | `pip install` needs network inside the container |
| Node.js/Express (greenfield); Node.js (brownfield) | `node:22-slim` | use `npm ci` when a lockfile exists |
| Java monolith, Java microservices (greenfield); Java (brownfield) | `eclipse-temurin:21-jdk-alpine` | JDK, not JRE — build/test steps compile |

If a job's declared version differs from the table (e.g. Python 3.11 instead of 3.13), swap the tag, not the family — check [reference/03-tech-stack-versioning.md](../../reference/03-tech-stack-versioning.md) for how versions get pinned.

**Never assume a client tool (`curl`, or any other verification-time dependency) is preinstalled in a verification image** — confirmed failure mode: an alpine-based image fails a health/API check with `sh: curl: not found`. Rather than hardcoding which package manager a given image family uses (stale the moment an image tag or base distro changes), detect-then-install at the very start of the start script's `command`, before anything else:

```sh
command -v curl >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  (command -v apk >/dev/null 2>&1 && apk add curl) ||
  (command -v apt-get >/dev/null 2>&1 && apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl) ||
  (command -v yum >/dev/null 2>&1 && yum install -y curl) ||
  (command -v dnf >/dev/null 2>&1 && dnf install -y curl)
}
```

**Never pass `apk add --no-cache`** — confirmed by direct reproduction: that flag is precisely what makes `apk` re-fetch the whole package index (`APKINDEX.tar.gz`, ~10s+ each time) and every package's `.apk` file on *every single call*, even though `docker_exec` already mounts a persistent cache volume at `/var/cache/apk` for any alpine-based image. Plain `apk add curl` (no flag) uses that mount automatically — no `/etc/apk/cache` symlink or other setup needed — and a second call becomes a sub-2-second pure cache hit with zero network activity. The same applies to `apt-get` on a Debian-slim image, for a different underlying reason: `docker_exec` also removes that image's own `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/docker-clean` policy (which deletes every downloaded `.deb` immediately after install, by design, to keep image layers small) and mounts both `/var/lib/apt/lists` and `/var/cache/apt/archives`, so the `apt-get update && apt-get install` idiom above already benefits from this without needing any change to the command itself.

The same check-then-install idiom applies to any other client tool a verification step needs (`jq`, a messaging-broker CLI, ...), not just `curl` — swap the tool name and its package name, keep the detection chain. This must never silently downgrade a report row to `Skipped: ...` — a missing tool is a start-script gap to close, not a genuine skip. If a check needs more than the app's own language client (normally a throwaway script in the app's own language, installed the same way its other dependencies are), install that client with the same idiom, in the same `docker_exec` call, before using it.

**This install cost repeats on every call to the same image, since only the package-manager dependency cache persists — not the OS package layer.** For an image reused many times in one job (a Java job's build/lint/boot-verify/fix-loop calls all share one JDK image, for instance), paying the detect-then-install cost on every single call adds real wall-clock time for no benefit after the first. Instead, the first time a job finds it needs a tool the base image doesn't ship, build a derived image once and reuse its tag for every later `docker_exec` call in the job, instead of the base image:

```sh
docker build -t sdlc-agent-toolchain:gradle-8.11.1-jdk21-alpine - <<'EOF'
FROM gradle:8.11.1-jdk21-alpine
RUN apk add --no-cache curl
EOF
```

Run this via `execute`, not `docker_exec` — it needs the host's own Docker daemon, not a disposable container — right after the job's `docker_pull` of the base image. Passing `-` as the build context (instead of `.` or a real path) makes this a context-less build straight from the heredoc, so it's immune to `execute`'s own working directory and never tars up an unrelated directory as build context. Tag it descriptively enough to show exactly what's layered on (base image + tool list) so it's obvious what a given tag contains; every subsequent `docker_exec` call in the job then names that tag as its `image` instead of the base one.

## Tools exposed by this skill

### 1. `docker_pull`

**When to use:** once, before the first `docker_exec` call for a given image in a job — makes the "install" step explicit and its failure (network, bad tag, Docker not running) visible on its own, instead of buried inside a lint/test step's output.

**Input schema:**

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "image": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Fully tagged image, e.g. 'python:3.13-slim'. Never 'latest'."
    }
  },
  "required": ["image"]
}
```

**Behavior:** runs `docker pull <image>`. Idempotent — a no-op if already pulled.

### 2. `docker_exec`

**When to use:** any install/lint/test/build command for a stack whose runtime isn't on the host — dependency install, `ruff`/`eslint`/`checkstyle`, `pytest`/`npm test`/`junit`, or a build step.

**Input schema:**

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "image": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Fully tagged image to run the command in, e.g. 'node:22-slim'."
    },
    "workdir": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Project directory to mount, as a path under /outputs/ (e.g. '/outputs/todo-api'). Never a path outside /outputs/."
    },
    "command": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Shell command to run inside the container, e.g. 'pip install -r requirements.txt && pytest'."
    },
    "network": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Optional. Join an already-running `docker compose` project's network (e.g. '<project>_default') so processes started by `command` can reach infra dependencies by their compose service name. Omit for plain install/lint/test/build calls."
    },
    "ports": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {"type": "string"},
      "description": "Optional. Publish ports to the host, each as 'host:container' (e.g. '8081:8081'), so a host-side test/report script can reach a service `command` starts. Omit for plain install/lint/test/build calls."
    }
  },
  "required": ["image", "workdir", "command"]
}
```

**Behavior:** mounts `workdir` read-write at `/workspace` inside the container and runs `command` there via `sh -c`, with `--rm` so the container is discarded afterward. Returns exit code, stdout, and stderr.

**Guidance:**

- One `docker_exec` call per logical step (install, then lint, then test) rather than one giant chained command — makes fix-loop failures attributable to a specific step.
- Only ever mount a path under `/outputs/`; never the agent's own project or the `aisdlc/` reference docs.
- If `docker` isn't on the host's PATH at all, this skill doesn't apply — fall back to whatever host-native tooling is available and say so, rather than failing the job outright.
- For a multi-module build/test command (any Java greenfield/brownfield mode), add `--parallel` (Gradle) so independent modules compile/test concurrently across the host's CPU cores within that one call, and `--build-cache` so Gradle's own task-output cache lives inside the same persistent `~/.gradle` volume this skill already mounts (see above) instead of going unused — a later call that only touched one module can then pull the other modules' unchanged task outputs straight from that cache instead of reprocessing them. Both are real wall-clock savings, no correctness change. This is unrelated to, and doesn't conflict with, the fix loop's own rule of re-testing only one failing module at a time (see [capabilities/build-quality-and-testing.md](../../capabilities/build-quality-and-testing.md)) — that rule is about which module you *target*, not whether Gradle may parallelize or cache the modules it does build.
- **Starting the app's own services for testing** is one `docker_exec` call whose `command` is a start script: use the job's derived tool image (see above) so `curl` and any other client tool the test step needs is already present, instead of repeating the detect-then-install idiom on every boot-verification/fix-loop call — then build once if needed, launch every service as a backgrounded process (`&`) inside that single call. **Poll each one's health instead of a blanket `sleep N`** — a fixed sleep always pays the worst-case wait even when a service is actually ready in a fraction of that time, and defaulting to `sleep` here (rather than writing out the poll loop) is the single most common way this step ends up slower than it needs to be:

  ```sh
  for port in 8081 8082 8083 8084; do
    i=0
    until curl -fsS "http://localhost:$port/actuator/health" >/dev/null 2>&1 || [ "$i" -ge 90 ]; do
      i=$((i + 1)); sleep 2
    done
  done
  ```

  Swap the health path and the iteration ceiling for the stack's own convention and the mode's documented timeout (120s Python/Node.js, 180s Java — see [capabilities/reports-and-deploy.md](../../capabilities/reports-and-deploy.md#boot-verification-every-mode-every-language)); the loop still exits the moment every service is healthy rather than always waiting the ceiling. Then either run the API tests from inside the same command (`curl` against `localhost:<port>`, since client and server share one container) or leave the processes running long enough (`sleep`/`wait`) for a host-side test/report script to hit the `ports` this call published. Pass `network` set to the infra's compose network so these processes can reach a database/broker by its compose service name. Because the container is torn down (`--rm`) the moment `command` returns, the command itself must be the thing that installs, starts, waits on, tests, and stops the processes — a background process cannot outlive the call that started it.

### 3. `docker_compose`

**When to use:** booting/tearing down only the project's **infra dependencies** — a database, a broker, a cache, anything from `docker-compose.yml` that isn't the generated application code itself (Oracle, Kafka, Redis, ...). These have no native-process alternative in this sandbox, so they're the one thing `docker compose up`/`down` is still for. Since one `docker-compose.yml` still declares both infra and the app's own (deliverable-only) service entries, name the infra services explicitly in `command` (`up -d oracle kafka`, not a bare `up -d`) — an unqualified `up`/`down` would also touch the app entries this mechanism deliberately bypasses.

**Every `up` in a job must be matched by a `down` before that job ends — mandatory, not an example-flow nicety.** Confirmed failure mode: a job brings infra up, finishes its own testing, and never calls `docker_compose(workdir, "down")` — the containers keep running (`docker ps` shows them `Up` long after the job's process exited) and keep holding their fixed host ports (Kafka's `9092`, Oracle's `1521`, ...). The *next* job that needs the same infra type then fails to bind those ports, and that failure shows up as that unrelated job's own boot verification timing out or its Kafka checks failing — a port conflict inherited from a previous job's skipped cleanup, misdiagnosed as a fresh bug in a project that never had one. See [capabilities/reports-and-deploy.md](../../capabilities/reports-and-deploy.md#boot-verification-every-mode-every-language) step 7 for exactly where this belongs in the sequence.

**Never use it to build and run the app's own services** (`up -d --build` bringing up each service from its own Dockerfile) as the mechanism for boot-verification / API-testing — that rebuilds a full image per service on every run and is the single biggest time sink in a job (docker image builds plus per-container test runs regularly account for the majority of a run's wall-clock time). The generated `Dockerfile` per service and the app-service entries in `docker-compose.yml` are flag-gated **deliverables** for whoever deploys the finished project — real, correct, and generated — but this harness's own verification never depends on them. See [capabilities/reports-and-deploy.md](../../capabilities/reports-and-deploy.md)'s "Boot verification" section for what the agent uses instead: a start script run via `docker_exec`.

**Input schema:**

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "workdir": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Project directory containing docker-compose.yml, as a path under /outputs/ (e.g. '/outputs/todo-api')."
    },
    "command": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Arguments after 'docker compose', e.g. 'up -d', 'down', 'ps', 'logs oracle --tail 100' — for the project's infra services, not its own generated services (see 'When to use' above)."
    }
  },
  "required": ["workdir", "command"]
}
```

**Behavior:** runs `docker compose <command>` with the real OS working directory set to `workdir` — never via a shell `cd` or a `-f <path>` flag.

**Use this instead of `execute` for every docker-compose command, with no exception.** `execute` runs on a real host shell, and passing a virtual `/outputs/...` path as a CLI argument to a native binary on that shell (e.g. `docker compose -f /outputs/.../docker-compose.yml up -d`) gets mangled into a bogus real path on this host (observed: `/outputs/foo/docker-compose.yml` → `C:\outputs\foo\docker-compose.yml`, which doesn't exist — the real path has a long prefix before `outputs`). That failure looks exactly like a genuine "file not found," so it's easy to mistake for the compose file actually being missing and move on — it's not; it's this specific path-argument bug. `docker_compose` sidesteps it entirely by setting the real working directory directly, never passing the path as an argument at all.

## Example usage flow

1. Detect the job's tech-stack mode (e.g. FastAPI) and look up its image (`python:3.13-slim`).
2. `docker_pull` that image once — and if the verification step needs a client tool the base image doesn't ship (`curl`, `jq`, ...), build a derived image on top of it once here too (see "Never assume a client tool is preinstalled" above) and use that tag for every later `docker_exec` call in the job instead of the base image.
3. `docker_exec` the dependency install (`pip install -r requirements.txt`).
4. `docker_exec` lint (`ruff check .`), then test (`pytest`) — per the AI SDLC's own STEP sequence for that mode.
5. On failure, feed the returned stdout/stderr into the fix loop (see [capabilities/build-quality-and-testing.md](../../capabilities/build-quality-and-testing.md)) and re-run only the failing `docker_exec` step, not the whole sequence.
6. If the project declares infra (a database, a broker, ...), `docker_compose(workdir, "up -d oracle kafka")` naming those infra services explicitly — never a bare `up -d` (which would also bring up the app's own compose entries) and never `--build` for the app's own services.
7. For the boot-verification / API-testing step, one `docker_exec` call whose `command` is the start script — build once, launch every service backgrounded, poll health — with `network` set to that compose project's network and `ports` publishing whatever the report/test step needs to reach from the host. Then test against those published host ports (e.g. `http://localhost:8080/...`) — see [capabilities/reports-and-deploy.md](../../capabilities/reports-and-deploy.md)'s "Boot verification" section for what "real HTTP request" testing actually requires, and for the full start-script/PID-lifecycle contract.
8. Tear down: kill the app's processes (the `docker_exec` call above already did this itself before returning, or a follow-up call issues the kill), then `docker_compose(workdir, "down")` for the infra.
